{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>הֲרַק אַךְ בְּמֹשֶׁה.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Genesis Rabbah 1:14) that אַךְ and רַק are terms of restriction, and here the intent in saying this is that the episode of Moses would imprint the blemish also upon the fathers of the world with whom HaShem had spoken — and so it is in the Midrash (Sifrei), and this is their language: ״But did He not also speak with the fathers?״ — end quote. Further, it is possible that the intent was to allude to two limitations that were in the prophecy of Moses: first, that he did not prophesy until he was eighty years old, as it is written (Exodus 7:7): ״וּמֹשֶׁה בֶּן שְׁמוֹנִים שָׁנָה... בְּדַבְּרָם אֶל פַּרְעֹה״, and it was at that time that HaShem spoke to him, whereas Aaron and Miriam prophesied at the ages of three and five, before Moses was born; and the second limitation, that on the very day he prophesied he was afflicted with leprosy, as it is written (ibid. 4): ״וְהִנֵּה יָדוֹ מְצֹרַעַת״ and so forth — which was not the case for them. Corresponding to this he said ״הֲרַק אַךְ״." "<b>וַיִּשְׁמַע ה׳.</b> It was necessary to say <b>וַיִּשְׁמַע ה׳</b>, to say that none other than HaShem heard — the intention in this is that they did not speak in the presence of any creature. It further intends to say that He heard it from them and not from Moses, for Moses did not lodge a complaint before HaShem in the manner of those who are insulted by their inferiors."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The abbreviation רַזַ״ל is resolved as 'our Rabbis, of blessed memory' per standard convention; 'ibid. 4' renders the Hebrew source reference שם ד׳ (i.e., Exodus 4)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:06.386304277Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}